/* MolData — reference tables for the Molecule tab: facts about each element, average bond enthalpies and
   typical bond lengths. Every value carries a source key; SOURCES holds the full citations.

   The tables are one plain-JSON literal between the JSON-BEGIN / JSON-END markers below, so that
   tools/check_mol_data.py can json.loads it and compare it with RDKit's periodic table. Edit values there
   only with a source in hand, then run the checker.

   ELEMENTS, Z = 1–118 (source key per field in `src`):
     sym, name (IUPAC spelling), Z, period, block, group (1–18), series ('lanthanoid' | 'actinoid')
         Layout: Lu and Lr sit in group 3 (d block); La–Yb and Ac–No are the f block and have group null.
     valence  valence-electron count. Groups 1–2 and 12–18: electrons in the outer s and p subshells (so Zn, Cd,
         Hg = 2, He = 2). Groups 3–11: outer s + (n−1)d electrons, which equals the group number (Fe 8, Cu 11).
         f block and Z ≥ 104: null.
     aw, awUnc, awKind, awStd, awAbr  standard atomic weight, CIAAW 2024 (the IUPAC "Atomic Weights 2021" report plus
         the 2024 revisions of Gd, Lu and Zr). awKind 'standard': aw is the full standard atomic weight and awUnc its
         uncertainty (I 126.90447 ± 0.00003). awKind 'conventional': for the 14 elements whose standard atomic weight
         is an interval, aw is the IUPAC conventional value (H 1.008, C 12.011, N 14.007, O 15.999, Cl 35.45,
         Br 79.904 …) and awUnc the abridged-table uncertainty. awStd is the CIAAW entry as printed
         ("[12.0096,12.0116]", "196.966570(4)"); awAbr/awAbrUnc the 5-figure abridged value. Radioactive elements
         without a standard atomic weight have aw null and massNumber = the mass number(s) of the longest-lived
         isotope (NUBASE2020, via CIAAW), shown in brackets as [222]; two or three numbers where the half-lives
         cannot be told apart (Tc [97] or [98]).
     mono     {A, mass, abundance}: the most abundant natural isotope (NIST representative composition, cross-checked
         with CIAAW 2024) and its atomic mass in Da (AME2020). null when there is no natural isotopic composition.
     en, enNote  Pauling electronegativity: Allred's 1961 revised values as quoted by WebElements. null where none is
         defined (He, Ne, Ar, Rn, Fr, Z ≥ 103) or only a range or guess exists (Pm, Eu, Tb, Yb); enNote says why and
         quotes the CRC Handbook value where it differs.
     enAllen  Allen electronegativity (Pauling-like units), main-group elements only (H, groups 1, 2, 13–18; not Tl,
         Po, At, Fr, Ra). Used to flag oxidation states whose assignment depends on the electronegativity scale (IUPAC's
         2016 definition of oxidation state uses Allen values). Source key MannMeekAllen2000.
     rcov, rcovSd  covalent radius and its standard deviation, Å (Cordero et al. 2008). Carbon also has rcovHyb
         {sp3 0.76, sp2 0.73, sp 0.69}; Mn, Fe, Co give the low-spin value in rcov and the high-spin one in rcovHS.
         null for Z > 96 (not covered).
     r1, r2, r3  Pyykkö single-, double- and triple-bond covalent radii, Å; null where not defined.
     rvdw, vdwNote  van der Waals radius, Å. src.rvdw names the set: 'Bondi1964' (main group, as tabulated by
         Mantina 2009); 'RowlandTaylor1996' (H = 1.10, not Bondi's 1.20); 'Mantina2009' (the 16 main-group elements
         Bondi lacked); 'Alvarez2013' (d and f block: radii from crystal contact distances, a different definition;
         Bondi's handful of d-block values are not used, see vdwNote). null where no source has one (Pm, Z ≥ 100).
     color    Jmol default colour '#RRGGBB'; null for Z > 109 (Jmol defines none).
     config   ground-state electron configuration with a noble-gas core, e.g. "[Ar] 3d5 4s1" (NIST ASD); configNote
         marks Z 100–108 as predicted; null for Z ≥ 109.
   BOND_ENTHALPY  [{a, b, order, kJ, src, note}] average bond enthalpies, kJ/mol, from ONE table: OpenStax Chemistry 2e,
         Table 7.2. Ten pairs it lacks (C=S, N=O, S=O, P=O, B–X) come from Huheey Table E.1 and say so. note gives
         Huheey's value where the two books differ. There is deliberately no aromatic C–C value (NOTES.aromaticEnthalpy).
   BOND_LENGTH  [{a, b, order, hyb, env, A, sd, n, sub, src, def, page, note}] mean bond lengths, Å, from Allen et al.
         1987 (organic crystal structures; X–H from neutron diffraction), with the sample s.d., the number of bonds
         n, the paper's substructure `sub` and the page. order 1.5 = aromatic / delocalised. hyb gives the carbon type
         of each end ('sp3', 'sp2', 'sp', 'ar' = carbon in a six-membered aromatic ring; null = any). S–H and H–H
         are not in Allen 1987 and come from Huheey. Like nitro, a carboxylate drawn with one C=O and one C–O− has
         env 'carboxylate' rows for order 1 and 2 as well as 1.5.
   SOURCES  full citation for every source key.

   API, window.MolData:
     element(symbolOrZ)  → the element object, or null. 'Cl', 'cl', 'CL', 17 and '17' all work.
     bondEnthalpy(el1, el2, order)  → {kJ, source, ref, note} | null. order 1, 2 or 3 (aromatic → null).
     typicalLength(el1, el2, order, hyb1?, hyb2?, env?)  → {A, sd, n, source, ref, sub, note, estimate} | null.
         order 1, 2, 3 or 1.5; hyb as above; env one of ENVIRONMENTS (e.g. 'ester', 'amide', 'alcohol').
         Use hyb 'ar' only for carbon in a six-membered aromatic ring (as Allen does); five-membered heteroaromatics
         are 'sp2'. A row written for one env is never used for another: with an env, that env's rows and the
         env-free rows fit (an env-free row is returned with a note saying there is no entry for the env); without an
         env, only env-free rows fit. The result's `env` names the row's env (null for env-free rows). With no
         tabulated match at all it returns the sum of Pyykkö radii with estimate: true and source 'estimate from
         covalent radii', but only for non-metals/metalloids (H B C N O F Si P S Cl Ge As Se Br Sb Te I) and only
         when one atom is C or H (not H–H) or both are the same element (not O–O, F–F). Anything else (metals, and
         heteroatom pairs such as P–F, Si–N, B–N, where the sums are 0.1–0.26 Å too long) gets null. Order 1.5 means
         aromatic: pass 1.5, not a Kekulé 1 or 2, for aromatic bonds. See NOTES.estimate.
     For aromatic bonds bondEnthalpy returns null: show NOTES.aromaticEnthalpy instead.
     ELEMENTS, BOND_ENTHALPY, BOND_LENGTH, SOURCES, NOTES, ENVIRONMENTS (the raw tables and texts).

   Built once from the downloaded sources and checked with tools/check_mol_data.py against RDKit's periodic table
   (RDKit is not the authority; its differences are listed and explained by the checker).                      */
